# Commit messages

## Subject (always)

A single conventional-commit line:

```
type(scope): short imperative description
```

- `type`: one of `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore`, `perf`, `build`, `ci`.
- `scope`: optional but preferred (e.g. `agent`, `frontend`, `env`). Drop it (`type: description`) when none fits.
- Imperative mood, lower-case after the colon, no trailing period, aim for <= 72 chars.

## Body (only when it earns its place)

**Default to subject-only.** Add a body *only* when there is a significant,
non-obvious WHY that a future reader couldn't infer from the diff — e.g. a
chosen trade-off, a workaround for an external bug, a constraint (RAM, prod VM),
or why an obvious alternative was rejected.

When you do add one:

- One blank line after the subject, then 1–3 terse lines or bullets. No paragraphs.
- Explain **why**, never restate **what** changed.
- Skip it entirely if the only thing to say is obvious (e.g. "added the missing env var", "renamed the function"). That is noise — leave it out.

## Examples

Subject-only (the common case):

```
fix(env): correct REQUEST_TRACING_ENABLED typo and default tracing on
```

With a justified WHY:

```
feat(agent): add Groq as a hosted chat-model provider

Lets the agent run on Groq's free hosted tier so small/low-RAM prod VMs
don't have to self-host a model. Default model avoids llama-3.3-70b-versatile,
which intermittently emits malformed tool calls (Groq 400 tool_use_failed).
```
